  "branch": "feat/subagent-commit-linkage",
  "base": "main",
  "title": "feat: link mid-task background-subagent commits to their session",
  "body": "Now I have enough context. Let me write the summary:\nProblem:\nWhen an Entire CLI session launches a background subagent (e.g., a parallel tool call), the parent session enters idle state while the subagent remains active. If the subagent commits code during this time, those commits were silently unlinked to any session — they carried no checkpoint trailer and no record in the session's metadata. This left background work invisible and unreplayable through entire resume or entire explain, degrading the checkpoint system for multithreaded agent workflows.\nSolution:\nThe changes implement live in-flight task tracking to link background subagent commits back to their parent sessions:\nIn-flight markers: Sessions now track background subagents in InFlightTasks, recording the tool ID, subagent identifier, transcript location, and launch time. These markers persist until the subagent signals completion via SubagentStop.\nCommit linkage eligibility: The prepare-commit-msg hook's fast path now treats IDLE sessions with active in-flight markers as \"eligible\" for auto-linking (alongside the existing ACTIVE-session case). This triggers trailer addition without requiring TTY interaction.\nCommit-time snapshots: After a commit lands, a new post-commit hook captures each in-flight task's state as a full (not incremental) checkpoint. The trailer points to this checkpoint, ensuring it contains the subagent's actual work instead of an empty placeholder.\nScoped trust: The marker is trusted only while IDLE (not after the session ends) and only while recent (24 hours old or less), preventing stale markers from ghosting unrelated commits into ended sessions.\nIntegration with condensation: Post-commit checkpoint condensation recognizes live markers the same way the trailer hook does, so checkpoints link consistently whether triggered by the marker or by file overlap detection.\nThe solution is backward-compatible — ENDED sessions never use their markers for linkage, and the system degrades gracefully when markers are missing or stale, falling back to file-overlap heuristics.",
